  out there  that when you guys armor up and look  like military  that's when they get wild as well they  get more militaristic when you get more  militaristic  no it's usually reactive usually we're  gonna uh suit up  after we say that the the temperature  has gone up okay  if they're yelling and screaming that's  fine but when bottles start coming i  gotta put the helmets on i got to get  the shields out to protect my guys  it's a show of force hard to miss for  protesters like shataja bird  what do you think when you see that i am  devastated i am disgusted we just  want justice for our people the same how  anyone else would  it's all we care for the police say that  they want to  have a light touch de-escalate do you  believe that  i mean you see them behind us right i  came today in flip-flops  ripped jeans in a shirt i have no need  or no desire to want to fight  to once we get pepper sprayed down to  want to be tied but i am prepared  because i will fight for my people by  any means necessary this  is our city monahan says he believes the  vast majority of people causing trouble  are coming from outside new york we have  a lot of encouragement from outside  agitators telling people  to go out and cause mayhem in the city  using i think this movement  for an agenda to end policing throughout  the country an anarchist  agenda you say end policing that's what  they do  all the signs that you see is defund  policing no more police  as monahan watch things unfold from the  ground deputy chief edward mullane had a  different view  from surveillance cameras feeding into a  command center at police headquarters  this is mob mentality this has nothing  to do with peaceful protests  trying to right injustice but these  people walking peacefully down the  street that's not bob mentality is it  well we don't like people walking in the  middle of the street we like the rights  of everybody else who wants to drive in  manhattan  but many americans clearly believe this  is the way to get attention  as they press on for change what role  does  racism play in these outcomes where you  have black men dying at a greater rate  than other populations at the hands of  police i don't believe racism plays a  role  in new york i can only speak for what  i've seen in new york city  and yet you've got these outcomes and if  those protesters heard you say that  racism doesn't play a role  that's why they're here that's why  they're angry they don't think you get  it  i would never say that we are a racist  police department absolutely not have  incidents happened  maybe there was a racist incident  something and that person  has been removed from this agency  absolutely  we all care about the communities we  work in we care  deeply in the minority communities the  cops that work there each and every day  yes you have a problem there that is  obvious to some people but chief members  of the nypd police force at least don't  want to name it  and if you don't name it it can be hard  to solve it it's worth noting gail  and one of the things that complicates  the situation here in new york is that  the majority of officers in the nypd are  minorities themselves it's a majority  minority department  and chief monahan points out that  officers in his department  they do frequently face repercussions  for inappropriate actions if there's a  bad cop they get rid of him he says  about 100 cops every year  are fired chief monahan i thought tony  touched a lot of hearts yesterday when  he knelt down with the protesters and  was hugging and engaging with them but  then on the other hand when people say  that racism isn't the issue or they  don't believe that's the issue it's such  a contradiction and it's so hard to  understand  that's why that's exactly as you point  out why people are in the streets  yeah you can bridge the gulf in part by  taking a knee and joining hands but if  you can't say it along with them it's  hard to move forward  but move forward we must

